INSTITUTIONAL FRAMEWORK
Article 79
(1) The Women's Empowerment and Child Protection System is the entire process of implementing women's empowerment and child protection carried out comprehensively, inclusively and integratively starting from the stage of report/complaint handling services, health services, social rehabilitation, repatriation, social reintegration and legal assistance up to repatriation and social reintegration for women and children victims of crime and violence in their social environment.
(2) The Women's Empowerment and Child Protection System aims to:
a. improve the quality of life of women, children and family quality;
b. increase the institutional capacity of women's empowerment and child protection including the development of gender and child data; and
c. provide special protection for children from various forms of violence and other discriminatory treatment.
(3) The Women's Empowerment and Child Protection System is carried out through:
a. providing access for women and children to education services, health and other strategic fields;
b. encouraging the involvement of women and children in the development process;
c. providing knowledge, skills, character values, morals and family resilience;
JDIH Kota Singkawang
d. encouraging programs that can increase women's independence in the economic, political, legal, socio-cultural fields as well as other strategic fields;
e. establishment of development and capacity strengthening of Women and Child Protection institutions including complaint service units for violence against women and children as well as legal aid services;
f. improving the quality of human resources of managers;
g. strengthening the institutional capacity of regional gender and child mainstreaming;
h. strengthening and developing gender and child data systems; and
i. conducting promotive, preventive, curative and rehabilitative efforts in accordance with Laws and Regulations.
(4) In the context of comprehensive, inclusive and integrative women's empowerment and child protection in the region, the Regional Government establishes:
a. P2TP2A or similar institutions that have the same functions and roles;
b. regional child protection commissions or other similar institutions to support supervision of the implementation of child protection in the region; and
c. other institutions according to needs and/or provisions of Laws and Regulations.

